1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:08,900
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:14,940
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

3
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:23,040
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

4
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:30,920
to stop killing each other" tax): Ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, is allocated to VICTORY Incentive

5
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:39,180
Alignment Bond investors to repay campaign funders. Ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, is allocated to

6
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:45,360
political incentives, specifically Incentive Alignment Bonds, to keep politicians aligned.

7
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:52,480
Eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, is available for Wishocracy to allocate.

8
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:59,940
How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight billion dollars?

9
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:08,340
If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists the way a dropped

10
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:12,600
ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

11
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,680
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

12
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:25,940
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

13
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:30,860
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

14
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:35,980
is like asking two hundred bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

15
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:40,560
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

16
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:48,250
On my home planet (also called Wishonia, because we named the system after ourselves, which your species would call "branding"),

17
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:53,640
we've been running Wishocracy for four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven years.

18
00:01:54,080 --> 00:02:01,400
It still works. How Wishocracy Allocates the one percent Treaty Fund: Decentralized Crowdfunding.

19
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:08,360
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

20
00:02:08,919 --> 00:02:16,380
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates funds from the one percent Treaty Fund across competing campaigns.

21
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:22,390
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

22
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:30,480
What your decentralized F.D.A. handles automatically (no voting required): Which treatments get tested: Companies register,

23
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:33,960
patients choose (capitalism, for once, doing something useful).

24
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:42,340
Which diseases get researched: Patients join trials for their conditions (people tend to be motivated about their own mortality).

25
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:47,780
Resource allocation within trials: Market prices and participant choices.

26
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:56,420
What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent Treaty

27
00:02:56,420 --> 00:03:01,730
Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

28
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:08,330
Decentralized Framework for Drug Assessment Development, one billion dollars per year proposal.

29
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:15,270
Epic electronic health records, or E.H.R., Integration Project, five million dollars one-time.

30
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:20,180
A.W.S. Infrastructure Services, three million dollars per year.

31
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:29,820
Security Audit Program, two million dollars per year. Alternative decentralized F.D.A., eight million dollars per year (competition!

32
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:38,520
novel concept for government!). Public Goods (things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that

33
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:45,500
don't sell pills). The Patient Trial Subsidies Program: eight hundred million dollars per year (automatic formula).

34
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:53,560
Off-Patent Drug Research: twenty billion dollars per year. The Rare Disease Initiative: ten billion dollars per year.

35
00:03:54,020 --> 00:04:02,700
The Negative Results Publishing Fund: five billion dollars per year (yes, knowing what doesn't work is worth billions; your

36
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:07,600
species keeps re-running failed experiments because nobody publishes failures).

37
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:16,200
Service Provider Bids (capitalism, but supervised). Data Storage Provider A: two billion dollars per year.

38
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:20,860
Data Storage Provider B: one point five billion dollars per year (competing).

39
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:27,680
The Pragmatic Clinical Trial Insurance Pool: ten billion dollar reserve.

40
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:37,860
How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns. No committee on Earth has ever looked at a list of ten thousand proposals

41
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:42,500
and made a good decision. Committees are where good ideas go to die of old age.

42
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:52,020
Instead, the global population votes using pairwise comparisons: "What's more important right now:" Framework for drug assessment

43
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:57,760
development versus the Alzheimer's prize? Epic integration versus security audits?

44
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:02,660
Rare disease research versus an alternative drug assessment framework?

45
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:09,260
Patient subsidies versus infrastructure spending? Millions of people make simple pairwise choices.

46
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:13,160
The algorithm aggregates preferences into funding allocations.

47
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:21,360
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

48
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:28,850
Why You Can't Just Use a Spreadsheet. Someone is going to ask, "Why not just write a formula?"

49
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:35,000
Because the decisions look like this: One. Multiple competing implementations.

50
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:39,120
Three teams want to build the drug assessment platform. Which one?

51
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:45,820
All three? Two? The one with the best logo? Two. Trade-offs between prizes.

52
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:53,060
One hundred billion dollars for aging reversal or fifty billion dollars for Alzheimer's plus fifty billion dollars for cancer?

53
00:05:53,760 --> 00:06:01,100
There is no "correct" answer. There is only what humanity collectively values, which is exactly what Wishocracy measures.

54
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:09,080
Three. Public goods allocation. How much for off-patent drugs versus rare diseases?

55
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:14,300
Markets won't answer this because there's no profit in it. Four.

56
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:19,250
Service provider competition. Which companies get contracts for what amounts?

57
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:25,800
Your decentralized F.D.A. allocates resources WITHIN pragmatic clinical trials.

58
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:32,400
Wishocracy allocates resources BETWEEN campaigns. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

59
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:41,700
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. The core of Wishocracy is a voting method so simple, even a congressman

60
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:49,920
could do it (though we'd recommend they practice first). It's called Aggregated Pairwise Preference Allocation, or simply,

61
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:55,820
the Pairwise Slider Allocation. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

62
00:06:56,340 --> 00:07:01,360
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

63
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:09,280
Instead of asking, "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now: decentralized

64
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:13,910
F.D.A. protocol development or security audits?" You make a choice.

65
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:17,720
That's it. You do this a few times with different random pairs.

66
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:23,220
It takes five minutes. Less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show, and considerably more important.

67
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:30,620
Millions of other people do the same. An algorithm takes all these simple, head-to-head comparisons and builds a real-time

68
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:38,440
ranking of infrastructure and public goods priorities. (Remember: Wishocracy doesn't allocate between diseases.

69
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:45,940
Patients do that by choosing which trials to join. Wishocracy allocates between *infrastructure campaigns* and *public goods*

70
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:51,780
that markets can't handle.). It's the wisdom of crowds, but without the crowds having to talk to each other.

71
00:07:52,940 --> 00:08:01,400
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning). When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical happens

72
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:08,820
(and by "magical" I mean "statistically inevitable," which is the only kind of magic that actually works): Random pairs

73
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:14,180
prevent gaming. You can't lobby five million people. Well, you can try.

74
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:22,200
Good luck. Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons.

75
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:27,870
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's medical priorities.

76
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:31,980
Google uses it to find cat videos. You'll use it to cure cancer.

77
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:40,860
Progress. Outliers cancel out. The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack" gets drowned out by the millions voting for

78
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:46,120
"cure my mother's Alzheimer's.". Wisdom of crowds emerges. Reliably.

79
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:52,020
Repeatedly. Boringly. Example with real numbers: Five million people vote.

80
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:56,200
Each makes twenty comparisons. That's one hundred million data points.

81
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:04,520
Algorithm processes them in seconds. Output: "Allocate forty percent to patient subsidy programs, twenty-five percent to

82
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:12,720
decentralized D.F.D.A. protocol development, fifteen percent to off-patent drug research, ten percent to rare disease initiatives,

83
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:17,870
five percent to security infrastructure, five percent to negative results publishing...".

84
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:21,000
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

85
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:28,460
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

86
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:31,960
and thus cancels out. This is called "the wisdom of crowds."

87
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:39,440
It works for the same reason that guessing the weight of a cow at a fair works: individually you're all terrible at it,

88
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:46,280
but collectively you're weirdly accurate. About half of the ten thousand civilizations I've worked with use some version

89
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:51,960
of this. The other half voted against it, which turned out to be the last thing they voted on.

90
00:09:52,880 --> 00:10:01,640
From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks). Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's"

91
00:10:01,700 --> 00:10:08,000
is a top priority. Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

92
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:14,960
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

93
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:24,220
One. A.I. Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller,

94
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:30,400
concrete, fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

95
00:10:30,840 --> 00:10:36,860
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

96
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:43,320
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

97
00:10:44,100 --> 00:10:51,100
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it

98
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:57,980
to medicine. Two. The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

99
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:03,620
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

100
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:10,880
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

101
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:19,460
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. Three. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

102
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:26,680
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

103
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:32,330
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

104
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:41,180
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

105
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:45,480
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.
